# Docker is a SECOND channel off this machine, and it had no exclusion list at all.
#
# The Dockerfile ends in `COPY . .`, so before this file existed the build context was the
# whole working tree — including `internal/`, which is 1.5MB of private research that the
# repo's first hard rule says must never be published. `git` was enforcing that rule and
# `docker` was not, so the corpus shipped to a third-party registry in the production
# image. Not served — the hub answers routes, not the filesystem — but present, and the
# only thing between it and anyone with registry access was that nobody had looked.
#
# EVERY PATTERN IN .gitignore MUST APPEAR HERE. That is asserted by
# `packages/core/test/repo-hygiene.test.ts`, not left to whoever edits one file next: an
# exclusion enforced in one channel and absent in another is the shape of every failure
# this repo has hit. Adding a pattern to .gitignore alone will fail the suite.

# ── mirrors .gitignore ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Internal only — research, competitive analysis, build decisions, run state.
internal/

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*-recovery-file.json
throwaway-keys.json

# Dependencies — reinstalled inside the image from the lockfile, and a host's
# node_modules may carry the wrong platform's native binaries.
node_modules/
.pnp
.pnp.js

# Build output
dist/
build/
out/
.next/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test / coverage
coverage/
.nyc_output/

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
*.swp
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# Local caches
.cache/
.turbo/
/lib/
contracts/out/
/cache/

# Sandbox litter
skills/*/Library/
skills/*/.cache/
skills/*/.bun/
skills/*/.npm/
skills/*/.config/

# ── docker-only, beyond what git excludes ───────────────────────────────────

# History is bulk and carries every past state of the tree. The image needs the working
# tree, never how it got there.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# `apps/web` is deliberately NOT excluded, even though the hub image does not run it.
#
# Docker resolves one `.dockerignore` per build context, and both services build from the
# repo root — so excluding `apps/web` here would make it invisible to its OWN Dockerfile.
# The cost of keeping it is a few hundred KB of source in the hub image (its node_modules
# and dist are excluded above). The cost of excluding it is a web build that fails, or
# worse, succeeds against a stale copy. One ignore file that is slightly generous beats two
# that can disagree.

# Docs, evidence scripts and agent config are not runtime inputs.
docs/
.claude/
.mcp.json
*.md
!README.md
